<drawables>
    <bitmap id="LauncherIcon" filename="launcher_icon.png"/>
    <bitmap id="RedMoon" filename="moon.png"/>

    <!-- Star sprites for the bright tiers, generated by
         tools/gen-star-sprites.py. Grayscale on opaque black: the field draws
         straight onto Theme.BG (always 0x000000) right after dc.clear, so
         nothing behind them needs preserving and no transparency is required.
         drawBitmap2's :tintColor scales by luminance, so white comes out at
         the star's full colour and the sparkle's 45% grey diagonals come out
         at 45% of it - one call for the whole sprite.

         automaticPalette="false" is required: drawBitmap2 refuses a
         palettised source ("Source must not use a color palette"), the same
         restriction the rotated second-numeral labels hit in renderLabel.

         Sizes are fixed pixels, not scaled(): Dial.scaled(2) and scaled(3)
         both round to the same value on venu3 (r=227) and venu3s (r=195), so
         one sprite per tier serves both watches.

         These are 2x the original 3/5/7 px sprites - arms of 2, 4 and 6
         pixels. The arms fade towards their tips rather than staying at full
         brightness, because a doubled one-pixel cross reads as a spider.

         Tier 0 is a blit rather than the two strokes it used to be. It was
         left as strokes on the strength of a benchmark that turned out to
         have been run with anti-aliasing off; on a Venu 3 a blit and a stroke
         cost the same, so one call beats two - and at arm 2 it would now be
         two longer strokes. See docs/track-perf.md. -->
    <bitmap id="Star5" filename="star_5x5.png" automaticPalette="false"/>
    <bitmap id="Star9" filename="star_9x9.png" automaticPalette="false"/>
    <bitmap id="Star13" filename="star_13x13.png" automaticPalette="false"/>
</drawables>
